From 0068f2ca8614a14ef425b1af1e15a4b51affe4c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:32:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: a group's own stdin redirection
 feeds its commands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the four stdin-feed fail-opens from PR #2316 review 5894173327. Each
ran a lifecycle .py/.sh on stdin that the guard never opened:

1. `cat f.sh | { { true; }; bash; }`: the inner `}` also closed the outer
   group's feed.
2. `cat f.sh | if true; then bash; fi`, `| while read l; do bash; done`.
3. `{ true; bash; } < f.sh`, `(bash) < f.sh`, `while …; done < f.sh`: the
   redirection follows the closer, so the walk had already read the
   group's commands and fed none of them.
4. `X=1<f.py python3`: the env-assignment pattern took the whole glued
   word, so the redirection hoist never split it off.

PR #2393 (group stack for nested braces and if/while/until/for/case, and
split_glued_redirections) already closes 1, 2 and 4; this adds their tests.
For 3, iter_line_segment_contexts now walks each line twice. The first walk
records, per group (keyed by open order), the stdin files named by the words
after its closer (`} < f`, `) <f`, `done 0< f`). The second walk feeds each
group from that redirection instead of the inherited feed, including the
command in the opener's own segment (`{ bash`, `{ { bash`). Feeds never
change which groups open, so both walks number them the same way. The
redirection words come from the guard's _split_redirections, injected as
stdin_redirections so lifecycle_guard_groups still never imports the guard.

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py. On main
(db5a056366, before #2393) 28 of its 29 feed cases fail (all four review
cases); the 13 mention/data cases pass on both trees.
